        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_016PneBtjyy3SQgCSMRYc6Ak",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022concepts/single-composition-point.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Single Composition Point\\ndescription: The pattern of resolving both credentials and runtime locations through one centralized decision point to prevent scattering and duplicated validation logic across the Hub codebase.\\ntimestamp: 2025-08-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - source-type/synthesis\\n  - concept/Single-Composition-Point\\n  - pattern/Centralization\\nconfidence: high\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Both credential-scoping and runtime-paths pages explicitly describe centralizing these concerns in the Hub startup path; the pattern is identical and intentional.\\\u0022\\nreview_date: 2025-11-14\\n---\\n\\n## The Pattern\\n\\n[[credential-scoping]] and [[runtime-paths]] reflect a common architectural principle: both centralize configuration and injection decisions at a single composition point in the Hub startup rather than scattering them across the codebase.\\n\\n### Credential Scoping\\n\\nThe Anthropic API key is resolved and injected in exactly one place: a local secrets loader reads the key at startup and injects it only into the spawned child process\u0027s environment. The Hub process never holds or accesses the key itself. This prevents any other part of the codebase \u2014 including the Hub\u0027s own process environment or any compromised Hub logic \u2014 from accidentally leaking credentials.\\n\\n### Runtime Paths\\n\\nEvery file-system location (wiki content root, database, secrets file, agent instruction directories) is also resolved in exactly one place: a single path resolver at startup validates configuration, auto-creates missing directories, and publishes the resolved locations. The rest of the codebase consumes these already-resolved paths rather than rediscovering them independently.\\n\\n## Why This Matters\\n\\nBoth decisions follow the same reasoning:\\n\\n1. **Single source of truth**: Validation logic and configuration precedence (environment variables, config files, defaults) exist in one place, not scattered across the codebase.\\n2. **Prevents duplication and inconsistency**: If credentials or paths were resolved in multiple places, different parts of the system could apply different validation rules or make different decisions.\\n3. **Eases extension**: Adding a new runtime location or credential type means extending the central resolver, not auditing the entire codebase for places that might need changes.\\n4. **Reduces attack surface**: Credentials and paths are validated and injected once, under controlled conditions, rather than being passed through multiple processes or accessed directly from ambient state.\\n\\nThis pattern suggests a broader architectural preference in Grimoire toward centralizing environment and configuration composition at startup, rather than allowing downstream components to discover their own configuration state.\\n\u0022}"
        }
