      "assistant_text": "I apologize for the technical difficulty with the index update. However, I\u0027ve successfully created the synthesis page and logged it. Here\u0027s the answer to your question:\n\n## The Relationship\n\n**Credential-scoping and runtime-path decisions reflect a shared architectural pattern: centralizing configuration injection at Hub startup.**\n\nPer [[credential-scoping]], the Anthropic API key is resolved and injected in exactly one place \u2014 a secrets loader reads it at startup and injects it only into the spawned agent\u0027s environment, never into the Hub\u0027s own process environment. This prevents the key from being exposed through the Hub\u0027s ambient state or accidentally leaked by compromised Hub logic.\n\nPer [[runtime-paths]], every file-system location the Hub touches at runtime (wiki root, database, secrets file, agent instruction directories) is resolved in exactly one place \u2014 a single path resolver at startup validates configuration, auto-creates missing directories, and publishes all resolved paths. Downstream code consumes these already-resolved paths rather than rediscovering locations independently.\n\n**Why both decisions follow the same pattern:**\n\n1. **Single source of truth**: Validation logic and configuration precedence exist once, not scattered across the codebase.\n2. **Prevents duplication and inconsistency**: Multiple independent resolution paths could apply different validation rules.\n3. **Eases extension**: Adding a new credential type or runtime location means extending one central resolver, not auditing the entire codebase.\n4. **Reduces attack surface**: Credentials and paths are validated and injected once under controlled conditions, rather than accessed directly from ambient state multiple times.\n\nI\u0027ve saved this connection as a new page, [[concepts/single-composition-point]], which consolidates this shared pattern and its implications.",
